media: ti: j721e-csi2rx: option to timestamp a buffer at the frame's start

The driver stamps a buffer with ktime_get_ns() in the DMA completion callback.
The shim opens a PSI-L packet for a frame at its CSI-2 Frame Start and closes
it with EOP at the Frame End (J722S TRM SPRUJB3D 12.6.1.4.6.1: "once the EOF
arrives at the FIFO output, all contexts of that type virtual channel ... will
receive a PSI_EOP"), so the transfer completes at the end of the frame, not at
its start. V4L2 asks for the time the first data byte was captured.

Usually that is one readout out and nobody notices. It stops being small when
the source keeps its CSI-2 frame open across the vertical blanking. Measured on
a J722S EVM with an IMX296 in external trigger mode at 30 Hz, polling the shim's
per-stream idle bit in CSI_RX_IF_CNTL against the trigger edges captured by the
CPTS: Frame Start lands 0.150 ms after the trigger pulse and Frame End 33.334 ms
after it, at the next pulse. One CSI-2 frame is one whole trigger period, the
1088 lines of readout sitting inside it after the exposure. So every buffer was
handed over, and stamped, a full period after the pulse that exposed it: 33.39
ms at 30 Hz and 50.07 ms at 20 Hz, tracking the trigger period and ignoring the
exposure.

With frame_start_timestamp set, a buffer carries the completion of the transfer
before it. For such a source that is 0.030 ms after the pulse that exposed this
frame (200 frames, 0.025 to 0.039 ms), which is where the exposure of a global
shutter sensor begins.

The driver cannot tell the two kinds of source apart on its own, hence the
parameter. The obvious test does not work: SHIM_CNTL has a per-stream idle bit,
but at the DMA completion the stream reads idle either way, because the callback
runs 30 us after Frame End and the next Frame Start is a further 120 us on. The
Cadence bridge's frame monitor timer interrupt (STREAMn_MONITOR_CTRL TIMER_EN
with TIMER_EOF clear, CSIRX_MONITOR_IRQS STREAMn_TIMER_IRQ) would give a real
Frame Start time and need no parameter, but the monitor only takes effect if it
is programmed before the stream starts, and its interrupt needs the bridge's
second interrupt line, which the driver does not request. Left for later.

This corrects the reported time, not the delivery: the transfer still completes
at the Frame End the source chose. No register moves that. The shim's PSI-L
framing is fixed - SOP on the first data phase after Frame Start, EOP on Frame
End - and its whole MMR set is CSIRX_ID, VP0, VP1, CNTL and the per-context
DMACNTX/PSI_CFG0/PSI_CFG1.

Against ti-linux-6.12.y, on top of the stock driver. Touches the same line of
ti_csi2rx_dma_callback as 0002, so the two need merging by hand if both are
wanted.

--- a/drivers/media/platform/ti/j721e-csi2rx/j721e-csi2rx.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/ti/j721e-csi2rx/j721e-csi2rx.c
@@ -64,6 +64,28 @@
 #define DRAIN_BUFFER_SIZE		SZ_32K
 #define DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS		250
 
+/*
+ * A source can keep its CSI-2 frame open across the vertical blanking. An IMX296 in external
+ * trigger mode does: measured on a J722S EVM at 30 Hz it sends Frame Start 150 us after the
+ * trigger pulse and Frame End 33.334 ms later, right at the next pulse, so one CSI-2 frame is one
+ * whole trigger period long. The shim opens a PSI-L packet at Frame Start and closes it with EOP
+ * at Frame End, so the transfer for a frame completes one trigger period after the exposure that
+ * filled it, and that is what the buffer used to be stamped with.
+ *
+ * With this set, a buffer carries the completion of the transfer before it instead. For such a
+ * source that is the start of this buffer's own frame, 30 us after the trigger pulse that exposed
+ * it. Leave it clear for a free-running sensor, whose transfer already completes at the end of the
+ * frame it carried.
+ *
+ * The driver cannot tell the two apart on its own. SHIM_CNTL has a per-stream idle bit, but at the
+ * DMA completion the stream reads idle either way: the callback runs 30 us after Frame End and the
+ * next Frame Start is 120 us further on.
+ */
+static bool frame_start_timestamp;
+module_param(frame_start_timestamp, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(frame_start_timestamp,
+		 "Timestamp a buffer at its frame's start, for a source whose CSI-2 frame spans the whole frame interval (default 0: at the frame's end, when the transfer completes)");
+
 struct ti_csi2rx_fmt {
 	u32				fourcc;	/* Four character code. */
 	u32				code;	/* Mbus code. */
@@ -111,6 +133,8 @@
 	u32				idx;
 	u32				vc;
 	u32				stream;
+	/* When the previous transfer on this context completed, 0 before the first. */
+	u64				prev_done_ns;
 };
 
 struct ti_csi2rx_dev {
@@ -709,6 +733,27 @@
 	writel(reg, csi->shim + SHIM_PSI_CFG0(ctx->idx));
 }
 
+/*
+ * The V4L2 timestamp for the buffer whose transfer completed at @now, and remember @now for the
+ * next one. See frame_start_timestamp above: with it set the completion before this one is this
+ * frame's start, which is what V4L2 asks for; without it the completion time is kept, as before.
+ *
+ * The first buffer of a stream has no earlier completion to report and keeps the end of its own
+ * frame, so it and the one after it carry the same time - one frame's end is the next frame's
+ * start. Every buffer after those two is stamped at its own frame's start.
+ *
+ * Called from the drain callback too, so that a frame nobody had a buffer for does not break the
+ * chain. Both callbacks run from the same dmaengine channel, so they are already serialised.
+ */
+static u64 ti_csi2rx_timestamp(struct ti_csi2rx_ctx *ctx, u64 now)
+{
+	u64 prev = ctx->prev_done_ns;
+
+	ctx->prev_done_ns = now;
+
+	return frame_start_timestamp && prev ? prev : now;
+}
+
 static void ti_csi2rx_drain_callback(void *param)
 {
 	struct ti_csi2rx_ctx *ctx = param;
@@ -726,6 +771,8 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	ti_csi2rx_timestamp(ctx, ktime_get_ns());
+
 	/*
 	 * If dma->queue is empty, it signals no buffer has arrived from
 	 * user space, so, queue more transaction to drain dma
@@ -811,7 +858,7 @@
 	 * TODO: Derive the sequence number from the CSI2RX frame number
 	 * hardware monitor registers.
 	 */
-	buf->vb.vb2_buf.timestamp = ktime_get_ns();
+	buf->vb.vb2_buf.timestamp = ti_csi2rx_timestamp(ctx, ktime_get_ns());
 	buf->vb.sequence = ctx->sequence++;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&dma->lock, flags);
@@ -1061,6 +1108,7 @@
 	ti_csi2rx_setup_shim(ctx);
 
 	ctx->sequence = 0;
+	ctx->prev_done_ns = 0;
 
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&dma->lock, flags);
 
